    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d258">

                <pb facs="0232" n="182" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg182"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 661.939/21</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-08-17T20:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 17,
                                1918, 8 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received August 17, 2.38
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your telegram of July 5, 5 p.m. In view of the complete control of
                    Blagoveshchensk and Khabarovsk by Bolshevik and enemy forces, the British
                    Government has instructed Legation to urge upon the Chinese Government the
                    necessity of prohibiting the passage of supplies thither by Sungari River. This
                    appears to be a particular measure of military necessity, as distinguished from
                    general embargo, and I beg to request authorization to support British
                    representatives if necessary.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >MacMurray</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
